# DESCRIPTION

virtio-gpu drives a virtio GPU (Virtual I/O Device 1.2, section 5.7), its 2D part, and serves the display class: `todhchai.display.device`'s `Display`, version 2.
devmgr(8) starts one for each device that matches its record (driver(6)), and mounts the driver's tree at `dev/display<n>` in its own, so a client with `mount /dev devmgr/dev` reaches it at `/dev/display<n>`:

```text
driver virtio-gpu
class display
match pci 1af4:1050
```

The driver takes the device's EDID feature if it offers it, and refuses a device that does not let the IOMMU translate its addresses (`ACCESS_PLATFORM`; QEMU's with `iommu_platform=on`).
It sends one request at a time on the control queue, a request the device reads and a response it writes, and waits for the answer for up to a second.
Its interrupt is MSI-X where devmgr gave it a vector, else INTx; the device's configuration interrupt comes on the same one.

`Display`'s calls:

: `outputs`
  The device's scanouts (`GET_DISPLAY_INFO`): each enabled one, and the first always, with its size; the refresh is 60 Hz, as QEMU's is.
: `edid`
  An output's EDID (`GET_EDID`), or nothing if the device has no EDID.
: `attach`
  A client's memory, at least `width` by `height` 32-bit pixels, rows packed, becomes a 2D resource the device reads: its pages are pinned through the device's BTI and given as the resource's backing.
: `detach`
  The resource goes and its pages are unpinned.
: `show`
  An output shows an image (`SET_SCANOUT`), or nothing for image 0.
: `flush`
  Each damaged rectangle of an image, clipped to it, is copied to the device (`TRANSFER_TO_HOST_2D`) and to the screen (`RESOURCE_FLUSH`); with no rectangles, the whole image.
: `present`
  Where the output already shows the image, a `flush`. Otherwise the damage is copied to the device, the output shows the image (`SET_SCANOUT`), and the whole image goes to the screen.
: `outputsChanged`
  An event to every client when the device says the displays changed (`VIRTIO_GPU_EVENT_DISPLAY`), which the driver clears.

radharc(4) is its client: it composes onto the enabled outputs.

Where the device offers virgl, blob resources and context init together, the driver also serves the GPU class at `gpu` (gpu-device-device(5), gpu-device-context(5)): the device's capability sets, and a context on one (Venus's is 4) whose renderer is the host's.
A context makes blobs, the renderer's memory for its objects or memory shared with it, and maps a mappable one at a window of the device's host-visible region, a BAR: the client gets a slice of a physical VMO over that window, cached as the host says, and the window is reused once the blob is released and no slice of it is left.
A context runs command streams (`SUBMIT_3D`), and a stream's syncs, the client's counters, are raised to their values when the device answers a fence on the stream's timeline.
The Venus client driver in loinnir(2)'s stack is its client.

# FILES

: `/dev/display<n>/display`
  The service.
: `/dev/display<n>/status`
  Read only: `interrupt MSI-X` or `INTx`; `scanouts <n>`, `edid` if it has it, and the enabled ones' sizes; with 3D, `3D: capsets <id>... , host-visible <n> MiB at BAR <n>`; then `images <n> flushes <n> failures <n>`, the images attached, the flushes done and the requests the device refused, `display events <n> interrupts <n>`, and with 3D `contexts <n> blobs <n> submits <n>`.
: `/dev/display<n>/gpu`
  With 3D: the GPU class's service.
  If setup failed, `FAILED: status <n>`, with a line before it saying why when the device bypasses the IOMMU.

